INT. APARTMENT KITCHEN - 3 A.M.

One lamp. A wailing baby monitor on the counter, its green bars spiking. NOREEN CALLAHAN-SINGH, thirties, in a faded T-shirt, jiggles a bottle on the stove, glances at the monitor, flips the burner off, and bangs the pot against the sink while her husband ANSELM CALLAHAN-SINGH, thirties, stumbles in with a blanket over his shoulder, bumps the doorframe, mutters a curse, grabs the bottle out of her hand, and shakes it near his ear to test the heat.

NOREEN
It's too hot.

ANSELM
It's fine.

NOREEN
Test it on your wrist.

ANSELM
(to the monitor)
Hold on, buddy. Daddy's coming.

NOREEN
(to the kitchen)
Why is nobody else awake in this building?

ANSELM
They have earplugs.

She snatches the bottle back, holds it under the cold tap, and hands it over. He nods once. She leans on the counter and rubs her eyes as the cries rise to a pitch, then a hiccup, then, somehow, a silence.

They look at the monitor. The bars flatten.

NOREEN (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Don't breathe.

ANSELM
(whispering)
I'm not.
